To use Boolean math to find vulnerabilities, security researchers look for inconsistencies in how a server handles logical truth statements. This technique is most famously used in Blind SQL Injection (SQLi) and Blind Command Injection testing. [1] 
When a server does not show error messages or data on the screen, you can force its database or interpreter to evaluate mathematical Boolean expressions ($True$ or $False$). By observing how the server responds, you can extract hidden information byte by byte. [2, 3, 4, 5, 6] 
------------------------------
## The Mathematical Formula
The testing process relies on the Identity Law and the Dominative Law of Boolean algebra: [7] 
$$A \land \text{True} = A$$ 
$$A \land \text{False} = \text{False}$$ 
In web security testing, we construct an injection payload where $A$ represents the normal server response (such as a webpage loading successfully with an HTTP 200 status code). We then append a logical conjunction ($\land$ / AND) along with a conditional statement ($P$) that we want to test:
$$\text{Server Response} = \text{Normal State} \land P$$ 

* If the condition $P$ evaluates to True, the entire expression simplifies to $True$, and the server returns the normal webpage layout.
* If the condition $P$ evaluates to False, the entire expression simplifies to $False$, causing the server response to change (e.g., loading an empty page, an error message, or a different HTTP status code). [8] 

